WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.607
The review queue contains patient 1003 and a NULL.

00:00:03.607 --> 00:00:11.052
For another patient such as 1001, PostgreSQL knows 1001 is not equal to 1003.

00:00:11.052 --> 00:00:16.882
But it cannot know whether 1001 is different from the NULL value.

00:00:16.882 --> 00:00:22.262
That unknown comparison prevents the full NOT IN condition from becoming TRUE.

00:00:22.262 --> 00:00:24.442
Run the query and observe the result.

00:00:24.442 --> 00:00:33.822
IN answers a membership question: is this value present in the set returned by the subquery?

00:00:33.822 --> 00:00:38.972
Be especially careful with NOT IN when NULL can appear in that set,

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:44.012
because three-valued logic can turn expected TRUE results into UNKNOWN.

00:00:44.012 --> 00:00:48.697
For anti-matching, NOT EXISTS is often easier to reason about.
